{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"async-iteration","title":"Async Iterators & for await","group":"Asynchronous JavaScript","order":47,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">When values arrive over time — pages of an API, lines of a file, chunks of a stream — async generators and <code>for await…of</code> let you loop over them naturally.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Async generators</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const fakeApi = (page) =&gt;\n  new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r({ items: [`p${page}-a`, `p${page}-b`], next: page &lt; 3 ? page + 1 : null }), 5));\n\nasync function* paginate() {\n  let page = 1;\n  while (page) {\n    const { items, next } = await fakeApi(page);\n    yield* items;\n    page = next;\n  }\n}\nfor await (const item of paginate()) {\n  console.log(item);\n  if (item === \"p3-a\") break;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>p1-a\np1-b\np2-a\np2-b\np3-a</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-down\"></i>for await over promises and streams</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const delays = [30, 10, 20].map((ms) =&gt; new Promise((r) =&gt; setTimeout(() =&gt; r(ms), ms)));\nfor await (const ms of delays) console.log(\"resolved\", ms);   // in ARRAY order, not finish order\n\nimport { Readable } from \"node:stream\";\nconst stream = Readable.from([\"chunk1\", \"chunk2\"]);\nfor await (const chunk of stream) console.log(chunk);\n\nconst collected = await Array.fromAsync(paginateSmall());      // ES2024\nasync function* paginateSmall() { yield 1; yield 2; }\nconsole.log(collected);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>resolved 30\nresolved 10\nresolved 20\nchunk1\nchunk2\n[ 1, 2 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between for...of and for await...of?</summary><div><p><code>for await…of</code> calls <code>[Symbol.asyncIterator]()</code> (falling back to the sync iterator) and awaits each value before the loop body runs, so it can consume async generators, streams and arrays of promises. It is only allowed in async functions and module top level.</p></div></details></section>"}